  "attributionNote": "Written by Aryabhata of Kusumapura in 499 CE, who states his date, his school and the tradition he follows in the work itself.",
  "description": "The Aryabhatiya is the oldest surviving Sanskrit work of mathematics and astronomy to treat both systematically: in four parts it gives the alphabetic notation of numbers, the arithmetic of squares, cubes, roots and proportions, the rules of interest and progressions, sines of arcs, the rotation of the earth, the sizes and periods of the sun, moon and planets and the calculation of eclipses.",
  "historicalContext": "Aryabhata's earth rotates, his sines are tabulated for astronomy, and his place-value system with a written zero reached the Arab world and beyond — the arithmetic algorithms of medieval Europe are called after his name. Commentaries by Bhaskara I and Someshvara carried the work into the astronomical tradition that his critics and successors shaped.",
  "availabilityNote": "Complete: all four parts (the ten-verse summary and the three divisions) in the Sanskrit text GRETIL publishes, streamed from GRETIL at read time, one part at a time. This is the original Sanskrit, not a translation.",
